"""Editorially reviewed question designs for chess and collecting."""

from review_angle_plans import audit, revise_domain


CHESS = """history_origins|How a Chess Rule Changed|Which documented practices led to one modern chess rule?|Compare dated rulebooks, games, and regional variants.
famous_examples|A Famous Game Reanalyzed|What choices made one recorded game influential?|Use the original score, contemporary analysis, clock context, and later study.
hidden_stories|Who Sustained a Chess Scene?|Whose teaching, organizing, annotating, and club work is omitted from champion stories?|Use club archives and attributed participant accounts.
beginner_explainers|Why a Move Is Legal|How do check, turn order, and piece movement determine one legal move?|Use a named rule set and simple board position.
advanced_concepts|Plans Depend on Position|Why is a familiar chess principle wrong in some positions?|Compare valid continuations and underlying positional features.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|A Disputed Historical Score|Can the moves or result of one old game be established?|Compare original score sheets, newspapers, databases, and inconsistencies.
technology_innovation|An Engine Changes Analysis|How did engine analysis alter interpretation of one famous game?|Compare human annotations, engine version, settings, and actual position.
people_personalities|A Player's Preparation|What evidence shows how one player prepared for a match?|Use notebooks, opening files, interviews, and opponent context.
major_events|A Tournament Turning Point|Which documented game or ruling changed one tournament outcome?|Read event rules, scores, appeals, and timing.
how_it_works|Evaluating One Position|How do material, king safety, activity, and threats shape a choice?|Analyze a bounded position and compare candidate moves.
rise_fall|An Opening's Reputation Changes|Why did one opening line lose or regain use?|Compare dated games, new analysis, and selection effects.
then_vs_now|A Historic Game Under Modern Eyes|What does modern analysis add and what context can it miss?|Compare original time control, knowledge, notes, and current engines.
future_developments|Testing a New Training Method|What evidence would show improvement beyond puzzle memorization?|Compare players, baseline skill, time, tournament results, and transfer.
myths_vs_facts|The Brilliant Move Legend|Was a famous move uniquely winning in the recorded position?|Check score accuracy, alternatives, contemporary judgment, and engine limits.
documentary_stories|A Chess Match in Records|How did one match unfold through games, rules, disputes, and public response?|Build a dated account from scores, official records, and interviews.
creative_brief|Designing a Chess Lesson|What should a learner understand after one annotated game?|Set level, concept, position, and transfer check.
materials_and_tools|Board, Clock, and Score Sheet|How do physical and digital tools shape one game experience?|Compare formats, time control, recording, and access needs.
process_breakdown|A Variation From Idea to Test|How did players investigate one opening novelty?|Trace preparation, game, analysis, and later responses.
composition_choice|Teaching With a Position|Which board diagram best exposes a tactical or strategic idea?|Compare versions and learner understanding.
collaborative_authorship|A Player's Analytical Team|Who contributed to one match preparation or published annotation?|Use credits, notes, and consented interviews.
adaptation_across_media|Chess Game to Broadcast|What changes when a game is narrated live or edited into a video?|Compare moves, timing, commentary, and audience understanding.
audience_reception|How Spectators Read a Game|What did contemporary audiences understand about one match?|Compare reports, attendance, broadcast context, and later reputation.
cultural_context|Chess in a Local Community|How did one club's rules, space, and membership shape play?|Use dated records and participant accounts without universalizing.
preservation_restoration|Keeping a Chess Record|What must be preserved beyond a move list to study a game?|Retain event, players, date, rules, clock, scores, and annotations.
rights_and_attribution|Credit for Chess Analysis|Who should be credited for one opening idea or teaching resource?|Trace publication, collaboration, and applicable reuse terms.
style_comparison|Two Approaches to One Position|How did two players solve the same strategic problem differently?|Compare position, time control, preparation, and continuation.
creative_failure|A Plan That Failed On the Board|Which assumption made a player's plan unsound?|Use actual move sequence and alternatives without claiming mental state.
field_rules_design|A Chess Rule in a Real Case|How did an arbiter apply one rule to a documented position?|Use the version of FIDE or local rules in force at the event.
field_player_strategy|Testing an Opening Idea|Does a proposed plan survive strong responses?|Analyze a named variation and compare serious alternatives.
field_difficulty_curve|From Tactic to Strategy|Which sequence helps learners move from simple patterns to planning?|Compare anonymized learner work and transfer positions.
field_community_norms|Conduct in Competitive Play|How did one tournament enforce fair-play and conduct rules?|Use published rules, official decisions, and appeal record.
field_accessibility_design|Chess With Different Access Needs|How do board, notation, and clock adaptations enable play?|Use player feedback and current competition rules for a defined need."""


COLLECTING = """history_origins|How a Collection Began|Which interests and opportunities shaped one private or institutional collection?|Trace dated acquisitions, records, funding, and original purpose.
famous_examples|A Celebrated Collection Revisited|What can one famous collection teach and what does it omit?|Compare catalog scope, provenance, display, and source-community views.
hidden_stories|Who Found and Cared for the Objects?|Whose sourcing, cataloguing, repair, or community labor is uncredited?|Use accession records and attributed contributor accounts.
beginner_explainers|What a Collector Records|Which details make an object's identity and history traceable?|Use one example to explain maker, date, condition, acquisition, and rights.
advanced_concepts|Value Beyond Price|How do rarity, provenance, condition, cultural meaning, and use differ?|Compare the same object through market, conservation, and community lenses.
mysteries_unsolved_cases|A Collection Item With No Provenance|What can be established when ownership records are missing?|Compare marks, receipts, archives, oral history, and unresolved gaps.
technology_innovation|Digitizing a Collection|Did a catalog tool improve access and care?|Compare metadata quality, rights, privacy, maintenance, and actual use.
people_personalities|A Collector's Decisions|Why did a named collector choose and reject particular objects?|Use acquisition notes, budget, correspondence, and context.
major_events|A Collection Changes Hands|How did a sale, donation, or return change one collection's access?|Trace transfer terms, condition, cataloguing, and stakeholders.
how_it_works|From Acquisition to Storage|What happens to one object when it enters a collection?|Follow due diligence, documentation, condition check, housing, and access.
rise_fall|A Collection Is Dispersed|Why was one collection sold or broken apart?|Compare financial, legal, care, rights, and institutional pressures.
then_vs_now|One Collection Across Generations|How did collecting purpose, cataloguing, and access change?|Compare inventories, policies, costs, and community relationships.
future_developments|A Collection Plan for the Future|What should a collector decide before accepting more objects?|Assess mission, provenance, storage, budget, rights, and succession.
myths_vs_facts|Rare Means Valuable?|Does scarcity alone predict an object's cultural or market value?|Compare authenticity, condition, demand, context, and price evidence.
documentary_stories|One Object's Collection Life|How did an item move from maker or user through owners to current holder?|Build a dated chain of custody and identify gaps.
source_trail|The Label's Origin Story|Where did a claim about one item first appear?|Trace catalogues, sales copy, scholarship, and corrections.
material_evidence|What the Object Itself Shows|Which physical features can test age or authenticity claims?|Use qualified material examination and avoid destructive testing.
chronology_dispute|When Was It Acquired?|Why do two records assign different dates to one item?|Compare invoices, catalogues, loans, and definitions of acquisition.
ordinary_lives|A Collector at Home|What time, space, and household costs sustain one collection?|Use consented accounts and records without romanticizing accumulation.
power_and_institutions|Who Sets the Collection's Scope?|How do donors, curators, markets, and communities influence what is kept?|Compare policies, funding, acquisition decisions, and exclusions.
trade_and_exchange|Following the Dealer Network|Through which sellers and intermediaries did one item travel?|Trace documented transfers, price where relevant, and due diligence.
language_and_labels|Names on Collection Objects|How do catalog terms affect how an item is understood or found?|Compare maker or community names with market labels and revisions.
maps_and_borders|An Object Across Borders|How do changing jurisdictions affect one acquisition's legal and ethical context?|Use dated law and provenance without inferring ownership from geography alone.
conflicting_accounts|Two Stories About an Item|How do seller and source-community accounts differ?|Attribute claims and test records without forced equivalence.
preservation_bias|What Collectors Chose to Save|Which everyday objects or groups are absent from one collection?|Compare acquisition patterns with community practice and archive gaps.
turning_point_test|Did a New Policy Improve Care?|What changed after a collection adopted provenance or storage rules?|Compare documentation, unresolved items, conservation, and access.
legacy_and_memory|A Collection After Its Founder|How did heirs or institutions interpret one collector's choices?|Compare inventories, donations, sales, and affected communities.
field_space_constraints|Storage Capacity Is Finite|How does available space affect which objects a collection can responsibly keep?|Compare object needs, environmental conditions, growth, and budget.
field_household_cost|The Price of Keeping Objects|What are the ongoing costs of one private collection?|Track insurance, storage, conservation, documentation, and disposal.
field_design_for_access|Who Can Use the Collection?|Which catalog, language, physical, or rights barriers limit access?|Test with defined users and source communities.
field_maintenance_burden|Care Over Decades|Which recurring tasks prevent deterioration of one collection type?|Compare condition reports, environmental monitoring, staffing, and costs.
field_material_lifecycle|An Object's Changing Condition|How does material degradation alter one item's care and use?|Trace condition, treatment, storage, and documented change."""


if __name__ == "__main__":
    revise_domain("134", "134_chess", CHESS,
                  "Chess through rule versions, recorded games, positions, training, fair play, community history, and accessible participation.",
                  "original game scores and event records, rulebooks valid at the time, player and arbiter accounts, dated analysis, and anonymized learner work",
                  "Name game, position, event, rule version, and date. Verify score accuracy, distinguish engine analysis from historical player knowledge, and follow current competition rules when relevant.")
    revise_domain("135", "135_collecting", COLLECTING,
                  "Collecting as acquisition, documentation, provenance, care, access, exchange, rights, and long-term responsibility for objects.",
                  "dated inventories and accession files, material and condition records, transfer documents, relevant collection policies and law, and attributed maker and community accounts",
                  "Name object type, collection, jurisdiction, and date. Document provenance gaps, separate market price from cultural value, and respect source-community rights and privacy.")
    rows = audit()
    print(f"Plans: {len(rows)}; reviewed question designs: {sum(r['status'] == 'editorial_question_reviewed' for r in rows)}")
